1
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:16,117
This is Conway Castle.

2
00:00:16,118 --> 00:00:25,000
King Edward I of England built this mighty stronghold in the late 13th century to help hold a recently conquered area of northern Wales.

3
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:32,000
Conway represents perhaps the pinnacle of medieval castle construction.

4
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:37,000
It appears formidable even today.

5
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:48,000
After more than 700 years, the sophistication of design is still clearly evident.

6
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:51,882
still clearly evident.

7
00:00:51,883 --> 00:01:03,880
Massive walls, projecting towers, crenellated top, heavily fortified

8
00:01:03,881 --> 00:01:13,254
entrance. Conway is only one of hundreds of castles that dot the European landscape.

9
00:01:13,255 --> 00:01:13,955
They

10
00:01:13,956 --> 00:01:19,780
They might look different depending on where they are or when they were built.

11
00:01:19,781 --> 00:01:23,340
But all castles were multifaceted wonders of construction.

12
00:01:23,341 --> 00:01:29,060
It's complex because it needs to fulfill so many functions.

13
00:01:29,061 --> 00:01:34,400
It needs to be oppressive, it needs to be defensive, it needs to be a home, it needs

14
00:01:34,401 --> 00:01:39,980
to be a palace, it needs to be used as a treasury, it needs to be used as a courthouse, it needs

15
00:01:39,981 --> 00:01:47,980
used to be used for a whole gamut of reasons and the architecture reflects all of these.

16
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:54,980
Castles were some of the most imposing structures ever built, withstanding both bloody assaults

17
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:58,980
and the test of time.

18
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:05,980
A well-defended castle would almost always win against anything but a very determined

19
00:02:05,981 --> 00:02:07,980
and very strong siege.

20
00:02:07,981 --> 00:02:15,220
In an unruly period, they represented order to some, subjugation to others.

21
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:22,220
The importance of the castle is as much to oppress the psyche of the population

22
00:02:22,221 --> 00:02:27,240
as it is to literally oppress the population.

23
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:33,320
Castles were homes, residences of kings or nobles.

24
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:36,960
That's what differentiates them from other fortifications.

25
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:44,980
They were built to protect a king's or nobleman's territory.

26
00:02:44,981 --> 00:02:50,980
Soldiers or men-at-arms lived there as well to guard the castle.

27
00:02:50,981 --> 00:02:58,980
It was a base where a lord could launch an attack, and it was a refuge from his adversaries.

28
00:02:58,981 --> 00:03:05,980
When most people think of castles, fortifications like Conway are what they think of.

29
00:03:05,981 --> 00:03:12,980
But the first structures now known as castles date back at least another 300 years,

30
00:03:12,981 --> 00:03:16,980
to the 10th century, and an even more unruly age.

31
00:03:16,981 --> 00:03:25,980
When times are unsettled, when there's not a strong central government, you have noblemen running around and bandits running around,

32
00:03:25,981 --> 00:03:30,980
and it's a natural thing to want to do to fortify your home, to protect yourself.

33
00:03:30,981 --> 00:03:36,000
The term castle is a derivation of the Latin word castellum, for fortress.

34
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:43,540
Many experts believe the first European castles originated in what is today France.

35
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:51,960
After the fall of the Roman Empire in the 5th century AD, Europe was a dangerous place to live,

36
00:03:51,961 --> 00:03:59,700
with weak and effective governments and constant raids from Vikings, Arabs, Berber pirates,

37
00:03:59,701 --> 00:04:02,040
and occasionally a disgruntled neighbor.

38
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:07,600
This turbulent atmosphere stimulated the rise of feudalism in Europe,

39
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,860
a system whereby a king granted territory to lords,

40
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:14,560
who in return owed the king allegiance.

41
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:21,680
The lords in turn granted smaller parcels of land to lesser nobles,

42
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:24,020
who owed allegiance to them.

43
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:27,680
Below these nobles were knights,

44
00:04:27,681 --> 00:04:30,140
the fighting class of the feudal system,

45
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:32,900
who in return for land grants

46
00:04:32,901 --> 00:04:35,080
rendered military service to their overlords.

47
00:04:38,780 --> 00:04:40,720
The introduction of feudalism

48
00:04:40,721 --> 00:04:42,440
combined with a weak central government

49
00:04:42,441 --> 00:04:44,840
incapable of adequately protecting its citizens

50
00:04:44,841 --> 00:04:47,800
gave rise to the first castles.

51
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:54,760
One of the earliest and most popular designs

52
00:04:54,761 --> 00:04:56,200
was the Mott and Bailey Castle.

53
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:02,880
Basically a giant mound of earth, a mott, with a wooden tower built on top.

54
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:09,160
The courtyard, or bailey, was enclosed by a tall timber wall.

55
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:18,680
This is a model of a Mott and Bailey castle, which is based on excavations at a castle built in the 11th century.

56
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:22,540
Here's a defensive ditch and a rampart.

57
00:05:22,541 --> 00:05:27,720
and then on top of the rampart was a timber wall called a palisade

58
00:05:27,721 --> 00:05:29,560
and it has crenellations.

59
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:33,060
There's evidence that these timber castles were plastered over

60
00:05:33,061 --> 00:05:36,040
so that from a distance they would look like stone.

61
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:40,860
Here's the classic mott, like an upturned pudding basin

62
00:05:40,861 --> 00:05:44,280
with a ditch separating the mott from the bailey.

63
00:05:44,281 --> 00:05:47,420
On top of the mott was a timber tower

64
00:05:47,421 --> 00:05:52,120
and this would have contained the private residential apartments

65
00:05:52,121 --> 00:06:01,160
Other buildings would have comprised stables and workshops and places for storage of food

66
00:06:01,161 --> 00:06:05,000
and a certain amount of small livestock, such as chickens.

67
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:08,360
Easy to build.

68
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:10,980
Martin Bailey castles spread across Europe.

69
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:20,580
William, Duke of Normandy, defeated the English king Harold II at the Battle of Hastings in 1066

70
00:06:20,581 --> 00:06:22,900
to capture the English crown.

71
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,340
He brought Mott and Baileys with him

72
00:06:30,341 --> 00:06:32,320
to help control his newly conquered land.

73
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,900
He built Lincoln Castle in 1068.

74
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,980
And like many surviving Mott and Bailey castles,

75
00:06:41,780 --> 00:06:43,440
it has evolved over time.

76
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:48,020
But the Mott still stands 40 feet high.

77
00:06:48,021 --> 00:06:51,380
That is a mot over there.

78
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:54,100
That's the original mot, that's the Lucy Tower.

79
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:57,580
It was the last bastion of defense.

80
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:02,840
If this castle was attacked, that's where we would escape to, into the actual keep.

81
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:08,280
The tower, or what eventually came to be called the keep,

82
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:11,880
also served as the castle owner's home.

83
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:16,680
Being higher than the enemy served several strategic advantages.

84
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:21,360
So builders tried to push towers and walls taller.

85
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:28,200
As the enemy came up with new tactics or weapons,

86
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:31,940
castle architecture evolved to face the new threats.

87
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:38,020
But the timber fortifications of the early medieval period

88
00:07:38,021 --> 00:07:39,340
eventually disappeared,

89
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:43,600
thanks largely to one enemy it couldn't defend itself against.

90
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:45,520
Fire.

91
00:07:45,521 --> 00:07:49,200
But castle evolution in general is very simple.

92
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:53,160
And somebody more intelligent than I has called it a bloody spiral.

93
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:57,420
In other words, fire attacks the wooden castle, build it in stone.

94
00:07:57,920 --> 00:08:00,220
Corners are structurally weak, build it round.

95
00:08:00,620 --> 00:08:03,620
People go through walls, build them thicker.

96
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:05,300
People go over walls, build them higher.

97
00:08:05,380 --> 00:08:07,380
In other words, it's all respond and defence.

98
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:12,800
As the Middle Ages progressed, so did castle architecture.

99
00:08:12,801 --> 00:08:19,280
simple earth and timber structures submitted to exponentially more complex strongholds

100
00:08:19,281 --> 00:08:27,200
stone fortifications sprouted from the countryside dominating the landscape and frequently the people

101
00:08:27,201 --> 00:08:34,040
around them they often talk about the beauty of strength the idea of looking at a building with

102
00:08:34,041 --> 00:08:40,880
impossibly wide ditches incredibly high walls which are massively thick with fortifications

103
00:08:40,881 --> 00:08:43,340
that seem impenetrable to the beholder.

104
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:46,000
That is the beauty of fortification.

105
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:50,340
But the real beauty is how they were built.

106
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,880
Stone and sweat, mortar and muscle combined

107
00:08:53,881 --> 00:08:57,140
to form structures that seemed like they would last forever.

108
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:06,940
In the early medieval period,

109
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:09,880
castle builders turned from wood to stone construction.

110
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:17,880
The more money the builder had, the more elaborate and massive the fortifications became.

111
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:27,880
Several of the most imposing castles in all of Great Britain were the result of one man's resolve.

112
00:09:28,880 --> 00:09:33,880
In 1283, Edward I, the King of England, conquered Gwynedd.

113
00:09:33,881 --> 00:09:35,880
Not Wales, just Gwynedd.

114
00:09:35,881 --> 00:09:39,880
Wales was a land of many different states, not unified politically.

115
00:09:39,881 --> 00:09:45,880
One of these states in the north and the west is called Gwyneve, and that is what Edward I conquered.

116
00:09:45,881 --> 00:09:48,979
Edward I was in essence doing a John Wayne.

117
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:53,880
He was moving west, the natives were hostile, and so he built forts.

118
00:09:53,881 --> 00:09:56,925
And that's what this castle is, it is a fort.

119
00:09:56,926 --> 00:10:02,880
Here we have a garrison of soldiers in a fort in the frontier with the hostiles outside.

120
00:10:02,881 --> 00:10:09,880
In the late 13th century, King Edward I ordered the modification or construction of more than

121
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:12,351
10 magnificent castles.

122
00:10:12,352 --> 00:10:17,500
Among others, Conway, Chirk and Caernarfon are enduring examples

123
00:10:23,640 --> 00:10:28,134
of Edward's dominating presence in northern Wales.

124
00:10:28,135 --> 00:10:31,640
To many scholars, the Edwardian castles

125
00:10:31,641 --> 00:10:34,280
represent the apex of medieval castle construction.

126
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:38,880
They're designed like machines,

127
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:42,660
every architectural detail devoted to defense

128
00:10:42,661 --> 00:10:46,280
as well as psychological intimidation.

129
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:51,000
Having stood for 700 years,

130
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:52,900
these castles are stunning,

131
00:10:53,820 --> 00:10:55,360
but the way they were built

132
00:10:55,361 --> 00:10:58,300
might be even more impressive to us today.

133
00:10:58,301 --> 00:11:08,320
there's nothing magical about the way they built they built by power manpower and they have three

134
00:11:08,321 --> 00:11:14,080
and a half thousand to actually do it earth moving equipment one and a half thousand men

135
00:11:14,081 --> 00:11:18,760
with shovels in their hand that was their earth moving equipment and boy could they move earth

136
00:11:18,761 --> 00:11:26,860
by utilizing thousands of craftsmen and laborers Edwards castles went up fast

137
00:11:26,861 --> 00:11:33,860
Conway was built in just four and a half years.

138
00:11:33,861 --> 00:11:39,260
Like subcontractors on a modern skyscraper, castle construction required over a hundred

139
00:11:39,261 --> 00:11:44,260
different crafts, including carters, carpenters, blacksmiths, and masons.

140
00:11:44,261 --> 00:11:48,413
The brilliant architect, Master James of St.

141
00:11:48,414 --> 00:11:52,660
George from Savoy, coordinated the remarkable

142
00:11:52,661 --> 00:11:55,660
construction effort.

143
00:11:55,661 --> 00:11:59,380
James designed several of Edward's castles as well as supervised construction,

144
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:04,120
which occurred simultaneously at different sites.

145
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:09,720
James' castles were big, broad, and tall.

146
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:17,880
Carnarfon boasts 13 towers, the tallest stretching 128 feet into the sky.

147
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:23,520
Building something this complicated took precise planning from the very beginning.

148
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:35,660
James Castles incorporated construction aids, such as scaffolding support holes or put log holes, into the structure itself.

149
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:45,660
The main construction of the castle, the building of the walls, was achieved by building a lowest wall to this height,

150
00:12:45,661 --> 00:12:53,240
then to put a log on the wall protruding to a vertical log here then

151
00:12:53,241 --> 00:12:59,600
scaffolding constructed and they could carry stones up the scaffolding placing

152
00:12:59,601 --> 00:13:05,920
them on the wall to allow the mason to stand on the wall and raise it further

153
00:13:05,921 --> 00:13:11,720
for again another beam to meet another horizontal and to have some more

154
00:13:11,721 --> 00:13:18,920
scaffolding and repeat the process as we climb up and up and eventually getting to the top.

155
00:13:22,440 --> 00:13:25,880
The walls at Carnarvon, which are seven to nine feet thick,

156
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:33,480
were built by constructing interior and exterior face walls with congruent stones

157
00:13:33,481 --> 00:13:39,980
and sandwiching smaller unshaped stones, rubble, in mortar in between.

158
00:13:39,981 --> 00:13:43,880
Filling the center with rubble saved time and effort.

159
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:49,880
For the face walls, masons cut large yet manageable stones.

160
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:55,000
The limestone came from the Isle of Anglesey, 10 miles away.

161
00:13:56,360 --> 00:14:00,580
To set the stones, they even had human or animal-powered cranes and winches.

162
00:14:03,540 --> 00:14:07,920
They used a plumb bob to make sure the finished structure ended up square and level.

163
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:13,820
But it took more than the masons' sheer will to hold stones together.

164
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:21,960
Castle builders used lime mortar, which had been used since Roman times, to keep the stones in place.

165
00:14:23,380 --> 00:14:26,420
Making lime mortar started with burning the limestone.

166
00:14:27,540 --> 00:14:30,180
That limestone is put into kilns.

167
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:35,000
From 45 miles down the coast come ships that carry coal.

168
00:14:35,640 --> 00:14:37,520
And the coal is put into the kilns.

169
00:14:37,521 --> 00:14:40,580
and the coal is burnt, the limestone is burnt

170
00:14:40,581 --> 00:14:43,040
and that produces quicklime.

171
00:14:43,620 --> 00:14:46,640
Now the quicklime is then mixed with sand and water

172
00:14:46,641 --> 00:14:48,360
and that makes lime mortar.

173
00:14:49,320 --> 00:14:50,960
And that's what holds this castle together.

174
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:56,560
At Carnarfin, masons used tough, light-colored limestone

175
00:14:56,561 --> 00:14:58,040
as the basic building blocks.

176
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:04,280
Around windows, arrow slits and corners,

177
00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:06,160
which demanded more detail work,

178
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:09,520
Builders used softer, conglomerate sandstone,

179
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,300
which was easier to work than limestone, but darker in color.

180
00:15:15,180 --> 00:15:18,460
The two disparately colored stones made an interesting design,

181
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:22,400
but the pattern was incidental to the actual function of the stones.

182
00:15:24,240 --> 00:15:30,440
Workers covered castle walls with plaster made of sand, water, quicklime, and sometimes horsehair.

183
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:35,760
This helped keep the lime mortar which held the castle together from washing out of the joints.

184
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:40,160
Then painters whitewashed the walls.

185
00:15:40,161 --> 00:15:48,160
The majority of those dark, grey, cold castles that we see today were in fact beautiful white jewels.

186
00:15:48,161 --> 00:15:56,160
And it gets more interesting because castles on the inside were all plastered and painted too.

187
00:15:56,161 --> 00:16:02,160
Just like our homes are plastered and painted, castles were multi-million pound buildings,

188
00:16:02,161 --> 00:16:05,160
and boy did they look it in their day.

189
00:16:06,160 --> 00:16:08,160
Another surprise.

190
00:16:08,161 --> 00:16:13,160
The Dark Ages weren't as dark as popularly believed.

191
00:16:13,161 --> 00:16:17,160
All castles had glass in their windows.

192
00:16:17,161 --> 00:16:21,160
We tend to think of castles as being bare and dark and cold,

193
00:16:21,161 --> 00:16:24,160
but like all churches, all castles had glass in their windows.

194
00:16:24,161 --> 00:16:27,160
Medieval glass is made in a very simple way.

195
00:16:27,161 --> 00:16:32,160
You take a blob of glass and a blowpipe, and you blow a bubble.

196
00:16:32,161 --> 00:16:35,160
You then put that bubble on what is known as a cooling table,

197
00:16:35,161 --> 00:16:36,458
cooling table and you roll it.

198
00:16:36,459 --> 00:16:39,140
You end up with something that's shaped like a can, all right?

199
00:16:39,480 --> 00:16:43,840
Then what you do is you cut off the two ends of the can, so now you have a tube of glass,

200
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:48,297
you slit it down the middle, and it folds out onto the cooling table.

201
00:16:48,298 --> 00:16:49,280
By that process,

202
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:55,160
you can only make small pieces of glass, and those small pieces of glass are always joined

203
00:16:55,161 --> 00:16:56,540
together with bits of lead.

204
00:16:56,541 --> 00:17:04,320
with an army of thousands

205
00:17:04,321 --> 00:17:08,220
James of St. George created a magnificent group of castles

206
00:17:08,221 --> 00:17:13,220
which Edward hoped would help finally suppress the Welsh

207
00:17:13,221 --> 00:17:16,700
to a large extent Edward was successful

208
00:17:16,701 --> 00:17:20,620
but the king's castles cost a king's ransom

209
00:17:20,621 --> 00:17:24,660
the 13 castles in the two wars

210
00:17:24,661 --> 00:17:31,020
cost him seven and a half years income he was bankrupt 14 years after this castle was started

211
00:17:31,021 --> 00:17:37,560
now I know this is an alien concept to the United States of America overspending on defense but you

212
00:17:37,561 --> 00:17:41,940
know it used to exist in Britain 700 years ago and some would argue it's a great old British

213
00:17:41,941 --> 00:17:50,700
tradition that continues to this day castles cost a lot but they weren't your average family home

214
00:17:50,701 --> 00:17:56,140
And there was definitely no welcome mat at the front door.

215
00:17:56,141 --> 00:18:01,900
They were designed to hold people out and decimate those who tried to breach their defenses.

216
00:18:01,901 --> 00:18:04,860
Castles were killing machines.

217
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:16,860
Castles may have had glass windows

218
00:18:16,861 --> 00:18:21,460
and pretty painted walls.

219
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:26,120
But that had nothing to do with their primary purpose.

220
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:31,000
The whole principle of the castle by the end of the 12th century

221
00:18:31,001 --> 00:18:35,800
is to establish means by which the maximum firepower

222
00:18:35,801 --> 00:18:38,960
can be brought to bear at any vulnerable spot.

223
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:44,720
Nearly every architectural element of a castle served a defensive purpose.

224
00:18:45,580 --> 00:18:48,500
The crenellation was designed to defend them.

225
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:53,680
They could stand behind the merlons, which are the parts that stand up,

226
00:18:54,160 --> 00:18:59,440
and when they wanted to loosen arrow, they could move to a crenell, which is the gap in the middle.

227
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:04,440
Castles were designed with multi-layered defences.

228
00:19:06,340 --> 00:19:10,380
An enemy had to confront several lines of defence before taking the castle.

229
00:19:10,381 --> 00:19:16,260
it all started with a great location

230
00:19:16,261 --> 00:19:24,300
before construction began castle planners looked for sites with existing natural obstacles

231
00:19:24,301 --> 00:19:33,180
a nice cliff or a solid peninsula worked well anything to hinder an assailant's approach

232
00:19:33,181 --> 00:19:40,580
In the flatlands where rocky cliffs didn't exist, moats were popular.

233
00:19:42,100 --> 00:19:48,280
A castle builder dug a ditch, or moat, around a castle as one more obstacle for the enemy to confront.

234
00:19:49,680 --> 00:19:56,220
The surrounding ditch, which did not necessarily contain water, could be quite broad, 30 feet or more.

235
00:19:57,720 --> 00:20:00,160
Sometimes sharpened stakes were embedded in the moat.

236
00:20:00,161 --> 00:20:05,180
But even if it didn't have stakes or wasn't filled with water,

237
00:20:06,140 --> 00:20:10,280
the charge up the embankment of the moat to get to the castle walls while being shot at

238
00:20:10,281 --> 00:20:12,720
was extremely difficult and harrowing.

239
00:20:14,060 --> 00:20:19,900
And no, there were never crocodiles, hippos, or dragons living in the moat.

240
00:20:22,100 --> 00:20:27,340
On the other side of the moat stood the wall and imposing towers.

241
00:20:27,341 --> 00:20:32,580
from these archers showered arrows on the enemy

242
00:20:32,581 --> 00:20:39,400
towers projected from the walls to give archers a clear shot at the base of the neighboring tower

243
00:20:39,401 --> 00:20:47,240
the edwardian castles were specifically built to take advantage of one of the most lethal weapons

244
00:20:47,241 --> 00:20:55,100
of the day the crossbow a weapon capable of piercing armor at close range

245
00:20:55,101 --> 00:20:59,980
An experienced bowman could shoot perhaps three to four bolts a minute.

246
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:07,500
Castle builders constructed loopholes, or arrow slits, in the towers,

247
00:21:08,100 --> 00:21:11,600
and sometimes in the walls, to protect their archers.

248
00:21:13,800 --> 00:21:17,040
A loophole was a thin vertical opening in the wall,

249
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:20,140
where the archer could take aim and fire.

250
00:21:20,141 --> 00:21:25,980
the slits played out inside the wall to give bowman maneuverability

251
00:21:25,981 --> 00:21:34,660
each loophole covers a specific arc so the archer is firing a bit like a modern machine

252
00:21:34,661 --> 00:21:41,560
gun defense and all the arcs intersect in a carefully designed castle so there's no point

253
00:21:41,561 --> 00:21:46,900
at which an attacker can as it were just simply walk through without paying a price

254
00:21:46,901 --> 00:21:53,560
The walls themselves could be massive, 20 feet thick at the base or more.

255
00:21:54,460 --> 00:21:57,400
Castle walls were wider at the bottom and tapered at the top.

256
00:21:58,480 --> 00:22:04,080
At the bottom of the castle wall, you'll often see a sloping mass of stones called a batter.

257
00:22:04,540 --> 00:22:08,500
Stones dropped from above, from the top of the wall onto the batter,

258
00:22:08,840 --> 00:22:11,820
will break up and spray shrapnel at the attackers.

259
00:22:12,400 --> 00:22:14,380
So it's a bit like dropping hand grenades.

260
00:22:14,381 --> 00:22:19,380
For attackers, there were only three ways to defeat a wall.

261
00:22:19,381 --> 00:22:23,380
Go over it, under it, or through it.

262
00:22:28,380 --> 00:22:35,380
But before a direct assault, one option was to bombard the castle with the medieval equivalent of Tomahawk missiles.

263
00:22:44,380 --> 00:22:51,380
A trebuchet is a long arm that has a heavy weight on one end and has a sling on the other end.

264
00:22:51,381 --> 00:22:54,380
What you load into there are typically large stones.

265
00:22:54,381 --> 00:22:59,380
You then use the counterweight to heave that stone against the walls of the castle.

266
00:22:59,381 --> 00:23:05,380
Stones were thrown at the top of the walls, the most vulnerable spot.

267
00:23:05,381 --> 00:23:17,600
If the castle didn't submit, the enemy could mount a direct assault.

268
00:23:19,100 --> 00:23:22,880
But those inside the fortification definitely held the advantage.

269
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:28,480
The most important defensive mechanisms in any castle are those that defend the gateway,

270
00:23:28,820 --> 00:23:32,360
because the gateway is the most vulnerable point of any fortification.

271
00:23:32,740 --> 00:23:34,540
It's the part that's not solid stone.

272
00:23:35,380 --> 00:23:40,380
If an attacker survived the onslaught of aero fire and made it through the moat,

273
00:23:40,381 --> 00:23:44,380
he could look forward to a warm welcome in the gatehouse.

274
00:23:44,381 --> 00:23:47,380
Let's pretend that we've actually done it.

275
00:23:47,381 --> 00:23:50,380
We've crossed the moat, we're standing on our bridge, which is down.

276
00:23:50,381 --> 00:23:54,380
We're lucky, because normally there'd be a great drop straight down to the moat.

277
00:23:56,380 --> 00:23:59,380
But from either side now we're threatened by those aeros.

278
00:23:59,381 --> 00:24:00,380
So we want to get out of this.

279
00:24:00,381 --> 00:24:03,380
And so we make our first major mistake,

280
00:24:03,381 --> 00:24:07,760
and we actually go into the gateway to get out of the fire.

281
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:12,520
Now, the first line of defence in the gateway proper

282
00:24:12,521 --> 00:24:14,920
is the portcullis.

283
00:24:18,860 --> 00:24:22,840
Now, people think portcullises were made of iron.

284
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:24,100
No, they're usually made of wood.

285
00:24:24,860 --> 00:24:27,800
The portcullis is raised and lowered in a groove.

286
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:29,220
Here's the groove.

287
00:24:30,080 --> 00:24:31,620
Portcullises were things you could look through,

288
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:32,800
things you could shoot through,

289
00:24:32,801 --> 00:24:34,560
but you couldn't walk through.

290
00:24:36,400 --> 00:24:38,040
Let's pretend we get beyond there.

291
00:24:38,580 --> 00:24:43,280
Well, beyond there, our next line of defence is this wooden gate.

292
00:24:43,580 --> 00:24:46,040
It's a reconstruction, but it's a fair reconstruction.

293
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:47,600
It's about five inches thick,

294
00:24:48,080 --> 00:24:50,880
and you attacking it would hit it with a big battering ram

295
00:24:50,881 --> 00:24:54,240
or potentially you would set fire to it.

296
00:24:54,380 --> 00:24:56,180
Now, look at the arrow slits to either side.

297
00:24:57,060 --> 00:24:59,800
From there, those crossbow bolts are going to be firing in.

298
00:25:00,120 --> 00:25:01,820
But now look up.

299
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:07,420
Above your head, small square holes, murder holes,

300
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:11,300
operated from above, and a nice big rock is posted down my head.

301
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:15,940
And then you make probably your last mistake.

302
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:19,040
You move on. You don't want to go back into there, into the gateway.

303
00:25:19,160 --> 00:25:23,100
You don't want to go in there, and so you move on into the centre of the castle.

304
00:25:23,480 --> 00:25:26,060
When you get into the centre of the castle, look what happens.

305
00:25:26,300 --> 00:25:28,700
Look up there. Look at those arrow slicks up there.

306
00:25:29,460 --> 00:25:31,980
And, of course, people begin to shoot in.

307
00:25:31,981 --> 00:25:35,680
And those that have been firing out now turn round and shoot inwards, and you're surrounded.

308
00:25:36,340 --> 00:25:38,800
There is nowhere to go. Do you want to stand here and die?

309
00:25:38,900 --> 00:25:40,580
Do you want to go back through that gateway and die?

310
00:25:40,640 --> 00:25:42,120
Or do you want to go through a door and die?

311
00:25:42,260 --> 00:25:44,820
And a castle is a trap, like a noose.

312
00:25:45,080 --> 00:25:48,580
The harder you pull, the further you go, the worse off you are.

313
00:25:52,260 --> 00:25:55,720
Despite the incredibly elaborate defensive characteristics of the castle,

314
00:25:55,721 --> 00:26:04,880
if a besieger had enough men supplies and time and was willing to make big enough sacrifices

315
00:26:04,881 --> 00:26:07,320
the castle would fall

316
00:26:07,321 --> 00:26:15,420
corners are structurally weak the place you ideally tunnel is of course the corners you

317
00:26:15,421 --> 00:26:20,460
drive a tunnel under the corner you prop it up with timber props you pack the tunnel with brush

318
00:26:20,461 --> 00:26:24,940
wood you light the brushwood the brushwood burns the props burn the tunnel collapses the corner

319
00:26:24,941 --> 00:26:26,700
collapses and the walls collapse, end of castle.

320
00:26:34,180 --> 00:26:36,240
A man's home is his castle.

321
00:26:37,820 --> 00:26:40,360
Or in this case, a castle was a man's home.

322
00:26:41,240 --> 00:26:43,540
And like any residence, it had the basics.

323
00:26:44,180 --> 00:26:48,040
Bedroom, bathroom, dining room, entranceway, dungeon.

324
00:26:49,180 --> 00:26:49,880
Dungeon?

325
00:26:51,140 --> 00:26:52,820
Dungeon is an interesting word.

326
00:26:52,821 --> 00:26:56,620
We think of a dungeon as being a dank hole where people were locked up.

327
00:26:56,820 --> 00:27:02,960
But the term really comes from the French word donjon, which is the name for the keep of a castle.

328
00:27:07,680 --> 00:27:12,020
Now, how it got that other meaning is not real clear.

329
00:27:13,160 --> 00:27:16,000
Virtually every nobleman had some occasion to lock somebody up.

330
00:27:16,500 --> 00:27:19,940
He might not have had a full-fledged prison, but there was always somebody he didn't like.

331
00:27:22,820 --> 00:27:27,220
This is what's popularly known as an oubliette or a bottle prison.

332
00:27:27,940 --> 00:27:29,180
There are two possibilities.

333
00:27:29,980 --> 00:27:31,660
Either it was used for storage,

334
00:27:32,140 --> 00:27:36,600
but it has to be said that the access is a bit awkward for getting your stores in and out.

335
00:27:37,100 --> 00:27:41,780
Or it was used as a rather unpleasant prison for retaining people.

336
00:27:43,020 --> 00:27:45,380
And it's formed with a cobalt vault.

337
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:48,500
That means the walls go up more or less straight

338
00:27:48,501 --> 00:27:53,940
and then the stones start to edge in to make a sort of beehive shape.

339
00:27:55,280 --> 00:27:58,820
Oubliet comes from the French word oublier, to forget,

340
00:27:59,800 --> 00:28:04,940
the implication being that they threw prisoners into these and forgot all about them.

341
00:28:05,840 --> 00:28:07,640
I can't see that happening.

342
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:10,260
Why would they want to forget about a prisoner?

343
00:28:10,840 --> 00:28:14,300
Either you're holding him for ransom or he's a political prisoner

344
00:28:14,301 --> 00:28:16,500
or he's a normal miscreant,

345
00:28:16,501 --> 00:28:21,880
in which case he's going to be hauled up for trial fairly soon and dealt with.

346
00:28:24,200 --> 00:28:26,240
Turcastle boasts a double-decker prison.

347
00:28:28,620 --> 00:28:30,480
We have two levels of dungeon.

348
00:28:30,760 --> 00:28:32,200
The upper level has a fireplace

349
00:28:32,201 --> 00:28:36,440
and is probably a room where you would have kept prisoners

350
00:28:36,441 --> 00:28:38,920
that you could sell for ransom.

351
00:28:39,560 --> 00:28:44,280
People that you wanted kept safe, but they were worth money.

352
00:28:44,281 --> 00:28:48,080
They could be gentry, they could be people captured in battle.

353
00:28:48,760 --> 00:28:51,540
The lower dungeon is a little more unpleasant.

354
00:28:54,240 --> 00:28:57,120
Fifteen French prisoners were kept in that dungeon

355
00:28:57,121 --> 00:28:59,800
for seven years after the Battle of Agincourt.

356
00:29:00,480 --> 00:29:03,000
There is light from three arrow slits,

357
00:29:03,460 --> 00:29:06,740
but even in the middle of the day, it is still pretty dark down there.

358
00:29:07,380 --> 00:29:09,220
We presume there'd been straw on the floor.

359
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:12,100
It always stays at constant temperature,

360
00:29:12,320 --> 00:29:14,600
about 54 degrees Fahrenheit.

361
00:29:17,500 --> 00:29:21,280
Besides being a jail, a residence, and an economic center,

362
00:29:22,020 --> 00:29:24,460
the castle also served as a courthouse.

363
00:29:26,080 --> 00:29:27,560
Nobles were the law of the land.

364
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:31,960
And although the accused might not spend much time in prison

365
00:29:31,961 --> 00:29:32,980
before his court date,

366
00:29:34,200 --> 00:29:37,700
the pre-trial questioning could be, well, torture.

367
00:29:39,220 --> 00:29:44,220
Torture, keeping someone alive for as long as you can,

368
00:29:44,221 --> 00:29:47,220
and inflicting often as much pain as you can,

369
00:29:47,221 --> 00:29:49,220
without killing them, of course.

370
00:29:49,221 --> 00:29:53,220
The rack, I think it was so famous because there's so many things

371
00:29:53,221 --> 00:29:56,220
that you can do while somebody is on the rack.

372
00:29:57,220 --> 00:30:00,220
Normally you're stretching them, you're stretching your limbs.

373
00:30:00,221 --> 00:30:04,220
At that point, you can burn them, you can beat them,

374
00:30:04,221 --> 00:30:06,220
you can cut them.

375
00:30:09,220 --> 00:30:18,140
There were all kinds of horrifying torture instruments used, especially during the time

376
00:30:18,141 --> 00:30:23,140
of the Spanish Inquisition.

377
00:30:38,480 --> 00:30:43,640
that crushed, stretched, poked, ripped, and were quite literally a pain in the rear.

378
00:30:47,080 --> 00:30:52,080
But the extent to which people were tortured, in the early medieval age at least, has been

379
00:30:52,081 --> 00:30:54,260
greatly exaggerated in books and movies.

380
00:30:55,580 --> 00:31:00,160
The torture was an accepted means of extracting information, although I think when every castle

381
00:31:00,161 --> 00:31:03,700
says, here's the room where the torture occurred, that's probably stretching things a bit.

382
00:31:05,720 --> 00:31:08,300
Few castles actually had torture chambers.

383
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:13,820
Most criminals were simply fined and released, or promptly executed.

384
00:31:17,460 --> 00:31:20,420
The dungeon was by far the worst guest room in the castle.

385
00:31:22,080 --> 00:31:24,780
But the rest of the fortified residents could be quite nice.

386
00:31:26,100 --> 00:31:28,640
And like any well-planned domicile today,

387
00:31:29,120 --> 00:31:31,560
the perfect home started with a good design.

388
00:31:32,360 --> 00:31:37,100
You never dared build a castle unless you had a water supply.

389
00:31:37,660 --> 00:31:40,180
Now, outside is a river, salt water.

390
00:31:40,360 --> 00:31:41,960
The tide comes in from the ocean.

391
00:31:42,440 --> 00:31:46,860
And yet, 10 yards away, happened to be a freshwater spring.

392
00:31:47,460 --> 00:31:50,100
If there had not been one, there would be no castle.

393
00:31:50,101 --> 00:31:56,720
This spring, this well built on it, it's 91 feet deep, and supplied the water for the castle.

394
00:31:57,680 --> 00:32:01,700
If the castle was besieged, it wouldn't last long without fresh water.

395
00:32:02,820 --> 00:32:04,760
Some had cisterns to collect rainwater.

396
00:32:06,200 --> 00:32:10,840
But if the castle wasn't being attacked, why drink water when you could have beer?

397
00:32:11,920 --> 00:32:17,180
This is the center of the whole social life and atmosphere of the castle.

398
00:32:17,181 --> 00:32:19,100
It is really in here.

399
00:32:20,100 --> 00:32:24,100
The king is in this castle. He wants everyone together in the banquet hall.

400
00:32:26,400 --> 00:32:28,520
Let us imagine a banquet now.

401
00:32:28,900 --> 00:32:32,460
Let us imagine a big fire in here and a big fire in there.

402
00:32:33,320 --> 00:32:38,560
Across that wooden wall, there is a high table for the king and the queen and their friends.

403
00:32:41,860 --> 00:32:45,420
These white walls are now covered in flags and bunting.

404
00:32:46,440 --> 00:32:49,120
Here are further long tables for the soldiers.

405
00:32:50,100 --> 00:32:57,703
And the servants are rushing in from the kitchens, and they're bringing the meat and the chicken, and the veal and the venison, the pork and the fish, the goose and

406
00:32:57,704 --> 00:32:58,404
the duck.

407
00:32:58,580 --> 00:33:03,220
They start eating at five o'clock, and three hours later, they're still eating.

408
00:33:03,740 --> 00:33:07,780
It's now eight o'clock, and the music starts from the minstrels here.

409
00:33:08,280 --> 00:33:12,580
The servants are rushing around with the wine and the beer.

410
00:33:12,581 --> 00:33:18,020
Now it's a cold evening, and now there's a big warm fire in here,

411
00:33:18,021 --> 00:33:22,820
their stomachs are bulging with the meat and the chicken and the veal and the venison,

412
00:33:22,821 --> 00:33:25,740
and they're pouring in the beer and the wine.

413
00:33:25,741 --> 00:33:28,420
Well, it's midnight, and the king says,

414
00:33:28,421 --> 00:33:31,620
bed now, come on, bed, because we're going to do this again tomorrow.

415
00:33:31,621 --> 00:33:33,820
And tomorrow, and tomorrow.

416
00:33:33,821 --> 00:33:37,540
And there's a banquet in here every night while the king is present.

417
00:33:37,541 --> 00:33:42,740
After a night of food and fun in the Great Hall,

418
00:33:43,040 --> 00:33:44,820
the king would retire to his bedchamber.

419
00:33:46,620 --> 00:33:50,520
So here we are in a living room in a castle.

420
00:33:51,120 --> 00:33:53,200
In the basement, nobody lives.

421
00:33:54,020 --> 00:33:55,760
Three living floors.

422
00:33:56,600 --> 00:34:00,400
The first thing you notice is this, the fireplace.

423
00:34:00,760 --> 00:34:01,800
Look at the size of this.

424
00:34:01,801 --> 00:34:04,260
But of course, you'd need something that big

425
00:34:04,261 --> 00:34:06,340
to warm something this big.

426
00:34:06,341 --> 00:34:11,020
The floor area of this single room is as big as any modern apartment.

427
00:34:11,280 --> 00:34:12,140
It's huge.

428
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:16,400
The walls would be plastered, the walls would be painted,

429
00:34:16,740 --> 00:34:21,280
and if you look over here, what you see is a lovely window seat.

430
00:34:21,820 --> 00:34:24,040
Now, the view from that window is stunning,

431
00:34:24,140 --> 00:34:26,260
out over the sea, out over the land,

432
00:34:26,880 --> 00:34:29,280
and you'd lean against your beautifully plastered walls,

433
00:34:29,600 --> 00:34:30,900
looking at your beautiful view,

434
00:34:31,160 --> 00:34:33,660
with your big fireplace burning in your hearth.

435
00:34:33,661 --> 00:34:37,920
After consuming so much beer at dinner,

436
00:34:38,540 --> 00:34:40,840
nature would undoubtedly be calling before morning.

437
00:34:42,320 --> 00:34:45,440
Fortunately, each living floor had at least one garter robe.

438
00:34:47,120 --> 00:34:49,840
Now, an army marches on its stomach,

439
00:34:50,660 --> 00:34:53,140
and one of the necessary byproducts of that

440
00:34:53,141 --> 00:34:55,440
is you have to have lavatories,

441
00:34:56,080 --> 00:34:57,700
bathrooms, washrooms, restrooms.

442
00:34:58,380 --> 00:35:00,120
Now, when we look in here today,

443
00:35:00,360 --> 00:35:02,760
what we see is a slate slab.

444
00:35:02,761 --> 00:35:06,800
but originally that would have been a wooden seat with a hole in it

445
00:35:06,801 --> 00:35:12,340
and beneath that slate slab is a shaft, a shaft like a chimney

446
00:35:12,341 --> 00:35:14,860
only in chimneys things go up

447
00:35:14,861 --> 00:35:19,580
and here, hopefully updraft allowing, things went down

448
00:35:19,581 --> 00:35:23,760
down into the river that came up to the castle's walls.

449
00:35:25,060 --> 00:35:27,840
While other windows in the castle might have glass

450
00:35:27,841 --> 00:35:31,120
the ventilation window in the garter robe did not.

451
00:35:31,121 --> 00:35:35,520
This helped keep noxious ammonia gas from rising up the shaft and collecting.

452
00:35:36,720 --> 00:35:42,980
So it might not have been deadly, but it was still foul-smelling and pretty cold in the winter.

453
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:51,920
But these amenities and fine accommodations only got better as time went on.

454
00:35:54,280 --> 00:35:58,760
By the 15th century, wealthy nobles couldn't decide whether to build fortifications

455
00:35:58,761 --> 00:36:01,400
or opulent palaces.

456
00:36:02,800 --> 00:36:05,900
The political environment, as well as new methods of warfare,

457
00:36:06,080 --> 00:36:08,720
eventually led to the castle's decline.

458
00:36:10,320 --> 00:36:14,980
But not before builders constructed some of the most stunning structures of all time.

459
00:36:24,160 --> 00:36:27,520
Castles represented the perfect fusion of form and function.

460
00:36:28,760 --> 00:36:35,760
Masterfully constructed, at once intimidating, yet beautiful.

461
00:36:35,761 --> 00:36:43,760
With the progression of time, the simple elegance of the Edwardian castle style was replaced

462
00:36:43,761 --> 00:36:48,760
by more ornate designs.

463
00:36:48,761 --> 00:36:55,760
In countries like Spain, castle architecture reached art form.

464
00:36:58,760 --> 00:37:03,240
These embellished Spanish fortifications were among the last true castles built,

465
00:37:08,600 --> 00:37:12,960
the product of a 700-year-old struggle between Christians and Muslims

466
00:37:12,961 --> 00:37:15,120
for control of the Iberian Peninsula.

467
00:37:18,660 --> 00:37:25,180
In 711, the Moors from northern Africa invaded and eventually conquered what is today Spain.

468
00:37:25,181 --> 00:37:35,900
The Christians fled north, but over the next seven centuries, Christian kings fought the Reconquista, the Reconquest of Spain.

469
00:37:38,080 --> 00:37:43,020
As the Christians slowly retook territory, they built hundreds of castles.

470
00:37:44,300 --> 00:37:50,360
The region of Castile gets its name from the numerous strongholds once covering the landscape.

471
00:37:50,361 --> 00:37:56,080
Here, even the Christian castles demonstrate a Moorish influence.

472
00:37:57,420 --> 00:38:01,520
One of the most magnificent is the Alcazar de Segovia.

473
00:38:02,900 --> 00:38:07,360
The Alcazar, that's really a demonstration of royal strength.

474
00:38:07,820 --> 00:38:10,080
It's a way of saying to the people in the countryside,

475
00:38:10,780 --> 00:38:12,860
it's the king who's in charge.

476
00:38:14,120 --> 00:38:18,100
Perched on a cliff carved over eons by two intersecting rivers,

477
00:38:18,101 --> 00:38:22,100
The Alcazar dominates the surrounding countryside.

478
00:38:22,101 --> 00:38:26,100
Well, this castle was built in the 12th century,

479
00:38:26,101 --> 00:38:30,100
but the construction lasted a lot of time,

480
00:38:30,101 --> 00:38:34,100
during all the Middle Ages, from the 12th century

481
00:38:34,101 --> 00:38:38,100
until the 16th century, and was built by the Christian kings

482
00:38:38,101 --> 00:38:42,100
to have here a court.

483
00:38:42,101 --> 00:38:46,100
The castle underwent many changes and additions over the centuries.

484
00:38:46,101 --> 00:38:48,600
traditions over the centuries.

485
00:38:48,601 --> 00:38:53,100
In a country inhabited by both Christians and Muslims,

486
00:38:53,101 --> 00:38:58,029
the Alcazar exhibited a blending of both cultural influences.

487
00:38:58,030 --> 00:39:01,100
Here, fortification met ornamentation.

488
00:39:01,101 --> 00:39:06,350
The workers in part, they were Muslims that they were living in this kingdom.

489
00:39:06,351 --> 00:39:07,100
They called

490
00:39:07,101 --> 00:39:07,876
Mudejar.

491
00:39:07,877 --> 00:39:15,340
Mudejar is a Muslim that had the religion, the culture, the language, but was

492
00:39:15,341 --> 00:39:17,860
living among Christian people.

493
00:39:20,080 --> 00:39:22,820
The Moorish influence reached inside as well.

494
00:39:24,420 --> 00:39:26,820
Every inch was elaborately adorned.

495
00:39:28,140 --> 00:39:31,480
Some of the greatest artisanship could be found on the ceilings.

496
00:39:32,840 --> 00:39:35,760
It took over a year just to restore this one.

497
00:40:03,026 --> 00:40:07,080
Architects and military strategists spent centuries refining the function of castles.

498
00:40:07,081 --> 00:40:12,080
until one invention blew a big hole in all that work.

499
00:40:12,081 --> 00:40:15,080
Why did the castle decline? Two reasons.

500
00:40:15,081 --> 00:40:17,080
First place, people invented gunpowder,

501
00:40:17,081 --> 00:40:19,080
and cannons could knock down walls.

502
00:40:19,081 --> 00:40:22,080
Took them time to perfect it,

503
00:40:22,081 --> 00:40:27,080
but in the end, a castle with high walls was no longer defensible.

504
00:40:27,081 --> 00:40:31,080
Second reason, well, the world changed.

505
00:40:31,081 --> 00:40:33,080
Centralised government became stronger.

506
00:40:33,081 --> 00:40:35,080
Kings became real kings.

507
00:40:35,081 --> 00:40:40,080
They didn't depend on lords anymore, and they didn't like lords having castles.

508
00:40:42,080 --> 00:40:49,080
By the 15th century, nobles in much of Europe started building luxurious homes instead of fortifications.

509
00:40:52,080 --> 00:40:56,080
Slowly, many of these mighty fortifications fell into disuse.

510
00:40:58,080 --> 00:41:01,080
Some were purposely torn down by cautious kings.

511
00:41:05,080 --> 00:41:10,960
Several Edwardian castles were partially dismantled after the English Civil War in the mid-17th century.

512
00:41:14,000 --> 00:41:18,320
Others were gradually deconstructed by villages needing building material.

513
00:41:19,240 --> 00:41:25,540
In the 19th century, there was a sort of rebirth of castles, not in the traditional defensive sense,

514
00:41:25,541 --> 00:41:30,340
but in the architectural sense, an attempt to build structures that looked like old castles,

515
00:41:30,420 --> 00:41:34,340
that captured the Romanticism that was perceived to have come from the Middle Ages.

516
00:41:34,341 --> 00:41:37,440
Many people in the 19th century wanted to recapture that period.

517
00:41:39,300 --> 00:41:45,140
Although castles may have disappeared, our fascination with them has never waned.

518
00:41:46,620 --> 00:41:50,180
The idea of things being beautiful because they're fortified

519
00:41:50,181 --> 00:41:55,420
is rather like the idea of a car being beautiful because it looks as though it goes very fast.

520
00:41:56,180 --> 00:42:03,060
That sense of theatre in castle architecture is a tremendously important element of castles.

521
00:42:03,061 --> 00:42:08,060
It's slightly dangerous and magnificent and powerful at once.

522
00:42:33,060 --> 00:42:34,460
